   "type": 0,
   "text": "Here's what I found. There are several WhatsApp improvements we should configure:\n\nCURRENT SETUP vs WHAT'S AVAILABLE:\n\nYOUR CONFIG NOW:\n- whatsapp: {} (empty - no customization!)\n- WHATSAPP_MODE=bot\n- WHATSAPP_ALLOWED_USERS=* (open to everyone)\n- No mention gating\n- No reply prefix customization\n- No free_response_chats configured\n\nIMPROVEMENTS TO MAKE:\n\n1. REQUIRE_MENTION + MENTION_PATTERNS\nThis is exactly what you just asked for - only respond when named. Add to config.yaml:\n\nwhatsapp:\n  require_mention: true\n  mention_patterns:\n    - \"(?i)\\\\bereb(us)?\\\\b\"\n    - \"(?i)@.*ereb\"\n  free_response_chats:\n    - \"YOUR_DM_CHAT_ID\"\n\nThis makes me silent in groups unless someone says \"Erebus\" or @mentions me. DMs always go through. free_response_chats lets you whitelist specific chats where I respond to everything (like Laura's egg group).\n\n2. REPLY_PREFIX\nCustomize or remove the \"Hermes Agent\" header on every message:\n\n  reply_prefix: \"\"\n\nSet to empty string to remove it completely, or customize like \"🔮 *Erebus*\\n──────\\n\"\n\n3. ALLOWED_USERS\nCurrently * (everyone). You could restrict to specific numbers for security.\n\nWant me to apply these config changes now? I'd recommend at minimum enabling require_mention with the Erebus pattern - that fixes the exact issue you flagged earlier.",
   "starred": false
  },
  {
   "id": 1746144,
   "key_id": "ACE72A85CB326E41631F9D3FD5074575",
   "ts_ms": 1775597582802,
   "ts_iso": "2026-04-07T21:33:02.802000+00:00",
   "from_me": true,
   "sender_jid": null,
   "type": 0,
   "text": "Done analyzing. Here's the summary:\n\n👀\n\nAlright John, deep dive complete. Here's the breakdown:\n\nENEVE CODE AGENT (Ecedo Software BV)\n\nA serious production system. 112K LOC Java, Quarkus-based. It's essentially a self-hosted Claude Code for enterprise teams — hooks into Bitbucket, Azure DevOps, GitLab, GitHub, JIRA, Confluence, Teams, and Aikido security.\n\nWHAT'S REALLY GOOD:\n\n1. ClaudeToolUseLoop — Their core agent loop is clean. Parallel execution of read-only tools, context compaction when approaching token limits, checkpointing every iteration so jobs can resume from failure.\n\n2. Guardrails — Blocked paths, command allowlists, max files/lines changed limits, shell command security validator (ported from Claude Code's bashSecurity.ts). This is production paranoia done right.\n\n3. Self-review loop — After making changes, the agent reviews its own diff against a checklist before committing. Smart.\n\n4. Learning extraction — Automatically extracts team preferences from code review conversations (\"we prefer constructor injection\") and remembers them for future reviews.\n\n5. Tool authorization gate — Each tool has isReadOnly(), isDestructive(), and isAuthorized() flags. Defence in depth.\n\nWHERE THEY COULD IMPROVE:\n\n1. 15+ method overloads on ClaudeToolUseLoop instead of a builder pattern\n2. Hardcoded to Anthropic — no model abstraction for using cheaper models on simpler tasks\n3. No cost controls (token tracking exists but no abort threshold)\n4. Monolithic — everything in one service, no worker separation\n5. No OpenTelemetry / distributed tracing\n\nWHAT WE CAN STEAL:\n\n- Parallel read-only tool execution\n- Context compaction with circuit breaker\n- Checkpoint + resume pattern\n- Self-review before commit\n- Learning extraction from conversations\n- Shell command validator security rules\n- Per-job-type tool sets (writable vs readonly)\n\nFull report saved. Want me to go deeper on any specific area?",
   "starred": false
  },
  {
   "id": 1746146,
   "key_id": "AC799508BDF3BBB23B71F83A8A2A0264",
   "ts_ms": 1775597582804,
   "ts_iso": "2026-04-07T21:33:02.804000+00:00",
   "from_me": true,
   "sender_jid": null,
   "type": 0,
   "text": "Got everything I need. Here's the UI analysis:\n\nAlright, UI analysis done. Here's the breakdown:\n\nENEVE CODE AGENT UI\n\nReact 19 + TypeScript + Vite 7 + Tailwind v4. 57K LOC across 179 files. Modern stack with TanStack (Router + Query + Store), Keycloak auth, chart.js + recharts for metrics.\n\nSCREENS (30+ pages):\n\nWork:\n- Dashboard (stat cards, needs-attention queue)\n- Jobs (list, detail with diff viewer, AI call inspector)\n- Plans (multi-phase execution plans with timeline)\n- Pull Requests (cross-repo PR overview)\n- Chat (streaming AI assistant with tool visibility)\n\nInsights:\n- Quality Reports\n- Coverage Trend\n- Scopes (JIRA issue readiness)\n- Review Metrics, PR Cycle Time\n- Developer Scorecard\n- AI Effectiveness + AI Stats\n- Knowledge Graph\n\nEngineering:\n- Architecture (Structurizr diagrams)\n- Log Analysis\n\nQA:\n- QA Scopes, Test Plans, Test Cases\n\nSOC II:\n- Security Issues (Aikido integration)\n- SOC II Audit\n\nSettings:\n- Repos, Integrations, Hooks (automation rules)\n- Job Configuration, Prompts (editable prompt templates!)\n- Memories, Knowledge Index\n- Customers, Users, Teams, System\n- Webhook Audit, Audit Log\n\nWHAT'S IMPRESSIVE:\n\n1. ThinkingPanel — shows Claude's tool calls in real-time with status indicators (running/completed/error), duration, and tool parameters. Users can SEE the agent working.\n\n2. SecretScanner — client-side detection of AWS keys, GitHub tokens, Anthropic keys, OpenAI keys, private keys, JWTs, DB URLs. Warns before sending, auto-redacts.\n\n3. ClarificationBlock — structured questions from the agent (text, single choice, multi choice, boolean). Agent can ask the user before proceeding.\n\n4. Streaming chat — SSE-based with TanStack Store. Content appears in real-time with mermaid diagram rendering.\n\n5. Editable Prompts page — all 39 prompt templates editable through the UI. No redeploy needed.\n\n6. RBAC — Keycloak + 4 roles (User/Staff/Developer/Admin) with 9 permissions. Menu items auto-hide based on permissions.\n\n7. Plan execution UI — visual timeline showing plan phases, each step's status, ability to approve/reject before execution.\n\nWHAT WE CAN LEARN:\n\n1. Tool call transparency — showing users what the agent is doing builds trust\n2. Secret scanning on the client — catch before sending, not after\n3. Editable prompts in UI — huge for iteration speed\n4. Job configuration per type — different models/token limits per job type\n5. Clarification flow — structured questions instead of free-text back-and-forth\n6. AI effectiveness metrics — tracking how useful the agent actually is\n\nFEEDBACK FOR IMPROVEMENT:\n\n1. No dark mode toggle visible (CSS vars exist but no UI control found)\n2. No WebSocket — uses SSE polling for streaming. WebSocket would be lower latency\n3. Chat is 1088 lines — single component doing too much, could decompose\n4. No error boundary components visible\n5. No i18n — English only\n6. Could add keyboard shortcuts for power users\n7. No offline/PWA support\n\nThis is a solid enterprise product. The combination of backend agent + this UI makes it a complete self-hosted Cursor/Claude Code for teams. Worth studying the patterns even if we don't copy the implementation.",
   "starred": false
  },
